1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:05,851
A coronary angiogram answers a question: is there a narrowing or

2
00:00:05,851 --> 00:00:10,246
blockage in an artery that supplies the heart muscle?

3
00:00:10,246 --> 00:00:13,664
In this example, the cardiologist reaches those

4
00:00:13,664 --> 00:00:16,594
arteries through the patient’s right groin.

5
00:00:16,594 --> 00:00:21,966
We’ll follow the instruments to the heart, see how contrast creates

6
00:00:21,966 --> 00:00:26,360
the images, and return to the groin for recovery.

7
00:00:28,855 --> 00:00:33,624
The heart muscle gets its blood supply from the coronary arteries.

8
00:00:33,624 --> 00:00:36,658
Plaque can build up in an artery

9
00:00:36,658 --> 00:00:39,260
wall and make the channel narrower.

10
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:42,294
An angiogram may be planned after a

11
00:00:42,294 --> 00:00:44,895
review of symptoms and other tests.

12
00:00:44,895 --> 00:00:49,664
In some suspected heart attacks, the team may need an urgent

13
00:00:49,664 --> 00:00:53,566
angiogram and may go on to treat the artery.

14
00:00:56,105 --> 00:01:01,845
For a planned angiogram, your team explains the test and reviews

15
00:01:01,845 --> 00:01:07,063
your medicines, kidney health and any previous reaction to contrast.

16
00:01:07,063 --> 00:01:11,237
Follow their instructions about food, drink and medicines.

17
00:01:11,237 --> 00:01:14,368
In the cath lab, staff monitor

18
00:01:14,368 --> 00:01:17,498
your heart rhythm and blood pressure.

19
00:01:17,498 --> 00:01:19,586
You are usually awake.

20
00:01:19,586 --> 00:01:25,325
The team can give medicine to help you relax if needed.

21
00:01:27,855 --> 00:01:32,595
The common femoral artery runs beneath the skin in the upper

22
00:01:32,595 --> 00:01:34,318
thigh near the groin.

23
00:01:34,318 --> 00:01:37,765
Here the team uses ultrasound to identify it.

24
00:01:37,765 --> 00:01:40,781
The skin is cleaned and the area

25
00:01:40,781 --> 00:01:43,367
is covered with a sterile drape.

26
00:01:43,367 --> 00:01:48,106
Local anaesthetic numbs the spot where the artery will be entered.

27
00:01:51,605 --> 00:01:56,994
After numbing the site, the cardiologist uses a fine access needle

28
00:01:56,994 --> 00:02:00,913
to enter the common femoral artery under ultrasound.

29
00:02:00,913 --> 00:02:04,832
The needle tip is shown inside the artery.

30
00:02:08,355 --> 00:02:12,568
A fine access wire passes through the hollow needle.

31
00:02:12,568 --> 00:02:15,844
X-ray helps the team confirm that the

32
00:02:15,844 --> 00:02:18,653
wire follows the main iliac artery.

33
00:02:22,168 --> 00:02:25,700
The needle slides back out over the wire.

34
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:28,791
Watch how the wire stays in the

35
00:02:28,791 --> 00:02:31,441
artery and preserves the entry path.

36
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:40,438
The team passes a temporary micropuncture introducer with its inner dilator

37
00:02:40,438 --> 00:02:42,423
over the fine wire.

38
00:02:45,918 --> 00:02:50,116
The fine wire and inner dilator are taken out.

39
00:02:50,116 --> 00:02:53,381
The outer introducer stays in the artery

40
00:02:53,381 --> 00:02:56,180
so the team can exchange wires.

41
00:02:59,668 --> 00:03:03,124
A larger exchange wire goes through the

42
00:03:03,124 --> 00:03:06,086
temporary introducer and into the artery.

43
00:03:09,605 --> 00:03:14,749
The temporary introducer comes out over the exchange wire.

44
00:03:14,749 --> 00:03:18,178
The exchange wire stays in place.

45
00:03:21,668 --> 00:03:24,830
The working femoral sheath and its dilator

46
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:27,541
slide in over the exchange wire.

47
00:03:31,042 --> 00:03:35,017
The team removes the exchange wire and the dilator.

48
00:03:35,017 --> 00:03:38,109
The working sheath stays at the groin.

49
00:03:38,109 --> 00:03:41,200
The team can take a separate groin

50
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:43,850
image to check the entry site.

51
00:03:47,355 --> 00:03:52,223
A longer diagnostic guidewire passes through the sheath.

52
00:03:52,223 --> 00:03:56,482
Live X-ray, called fluoroscopy, lets the cardiologist

53
00:03:56,482 --> 00:04:00,133
see where the wire is going.

54
00:04:03,668 --> 00:04:07,907
The diagnostic catheter advances over the long guidewire.

55
00:04:07,907 --> 00:04:13,206
The short sheath remains where the team entered the artery.

56
00:04:16,730 --> 00:04:21,766
The wire and catheter travel through the right external and common

57
00:04:21,766 --> 00:04:25,886
iliac arteries, then up the abdominal and chest aorta.

58
00:04:25,886 --> 00:04:30,922
They round the aortic arch toward the aortic root, where the

59
00:04:30,922 --> 00:04:34,127
coronary arteries begin above the aortic valve.

60
00:04:39,668 --> 00:04:42,522
At the aortic root, the long

61
00:04:42,522 --> 00:04:45,377
guidewire is withdrawn from the catheter.

62
00:04:45,377 --> 00:04:50,611
Here the curved catheter tip is positioned at the left coronary

63
00:04:50,611 --> 00:04:52,990
opening, above the aortic valve.

64
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:01,678
The cardiologist injects X-ray contrast through the diagnostic catheter.

65
00:05:01,678 --> 00:05:06,809
For a moment, it outlines the channel that blood travels through.

66
00:05:06,809 --> 00:05:11,940
Watch the left main artery fill and then see two of

67
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:14,738
its main branches come into view.

68
00:05:14,738 --> 00:05:18,936
Now look at the same moment on the X-ray.

69
00:05:18,936 --> 00:05:23,601
The dark branching lines show where the contrast is flowing.

70
00:05:29,105 --> 00:05:32,329
In this groin example, the team uses

71
00:05:32,329 --> 00:05:35,552
a different catheter for the right artery.

72
00:05:35,552 --> 00:05:38,315
The left catheter moves back from

73
00:05:38,315 --> 00:05:41,079
the coronary opening and is withdrawn.

74
00:05:41,079 --> 00:05:44,302
The working sheath stays at the groin.

75
00:05:47,792 --> 00:05:52,936
The right diagnostic catheter follows a guidewire up through the aorta.

76
00:05:52,936 --> 00:05:58,080
The team withdraws the wire and positions the catheter tip at

77
00:05:58,080 --> 00:05:59,950
the right coronary opening.

78
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:08,507
The cardiologist injects contrast separately at the right coronary opening.

79
00:06:08,507 --> 00:06:14,036
The right coronary artery and its branches briefly appear on X-ray.

80
00:06:14,036 --> 00:06:19,566
The imaging arm can move around the patient to record the

81
00:06:19,566 --> 00:06:21,577
arteries from different angles.

82
00:06:27,105 --> 00:06:29,989
On this example image, the contrast-filled

83
00:06:29,989 --> 00:06:32,873
channel is thinner at one spot.

84
00:06:32,873 --> 00:06:38,160
Another angle helps the cardiologist check where the narrowing is and

85
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:40,083
how severe it looks.

86
00:06:40,083 --> 00:06:45,370
Sometimes a pressure measurement or imaging from inside the artery helps

87
00:06:45,370 --> 00:06:48,254
answer what the angiogram alone cannot.

88
00:06:48,254 --> 00:06:53,542
If no major blockage is visible and symptoms persist, the team

89
00:06:53,542 --> 00:06:55,945
may look for other causes.

90
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:05,896
The next step depends on the findings, the symptoms, and whether

91
00:07:05,896 --> 00:07:07,866
the situation is urgent.

92
00:07:07,866 --> 00:07:11,312
It may be medicines, another test, angioplasty,

93
00:07:11,312 --> 00:07:14,266
or a discussion about bypass surgery.

94
00:07:14,266 --> 00:07:17,713
Angioplasty uses a balloon and may place

95
00:07:17,713 --> 00:07:20,667
a stent to treat a narrowing.

96
00:07:20,667 --> 00:07:23,622
In selected cases the team can

97
00:07:23,622 --> 00:07:26,576
do that in the same session.

98
00:07:26,576 --> 00:07:29,530
Here, the test ends after imaging.

99
00:07:33,042 --> 00:07:36,652
After the images are complete, the cardiologist

100
00:07:36,652 --> 00:07:40,261
withdraws the diagnostic catheter through the sheath.

101
00:07:40,261 --> 00:07:44,385
The sheath is still at the access site.

102
00:07:47,918 --> 00:07:52,988
For manual closure, the team removes the groin sheath and presses

103
00:07:52,988 --> 00:07:56,676
firmly over the entry site to control bleeding.

104
00:08:00,168 --> 00:08:05,474
In selected cases, the team uses a closure device, such as

105
00:08:05,474 --> 00:08:07,886
a plug or stitch system.

106
00:08:07,886 --> 00:08:10,781
Staff check the site and leg

107
00:08:10,781 --> 00:08:13,675
circulation before the patient gets up.

108
00:08:17,168 --> 00:08:22,018
Staff check your pulse, blood pressure, groin and leg circulation.

109
00:08:22,018 --> 00:08:27,353
You may need to lie flat with the leg straight while

110
00:08:27,353 --> 00:08:29,293
the site is monitored.

111
00:08:29,293 --> 00:08:32,688
After an uncomplicated planned test, many patients

112
00:08:32,688 --> 00:08:35,598
can go home the same day.

113
00:08:35,598 --> 00:08:40,933
Ask what the angiogram showed, when you can walk, lift or

114
00:08:40,933 --> 00:08:44,328
drive, and whom to contact with questions.

115
00:08:47,855 --> 00:08:53,997
Follow the written instructions for the dressing, activity, medicines and follow-up.

116
00:08:53,997 --> 00:08:58,464
A small bruise or mild soreness can happen.

117
00:08:58,464 --> 00:09:02,372
Contact the care team promptly for increasing

118
00:09:02,372 --> 00:09:05,722
redness or pain, drainage or fever.

119
00:09:09,230 --> 00:09:14,802
Call local emergency services for heavy bleeding or bleeding that continues

120
00:09:14,802 --> 00:09:20,373
despite firm pressure, rapidly growing swelling, a newly cold or numb

121
00:09:20,373 --> 00:09:24,932
foot, new chest pressure or sudden shortness of breath.

122
00:09:29,418 --> 00:09:33,926
The groin gives the team a route to the heart.

123
00:09:33,926 --> 00:09:38,885
The catheter delivers contrast, the X-rays show the coronary arteries, and

124
00:09:38,885 --> 00:09:42,041
the images help guide the next decision.

125
00:09:42,041 --> 00:09:47,000
Before you go home, make sure you know your result and

126
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,254
what to do during recovery.
